{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LSiM7rP1igPdFr5L1pyj5s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FJcotXASHd7kdeVDZSpUAd","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Provide an independent, blind review. Do NOT speculate about how good/bad the paper is in advance — judge purely on the evidence.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3.5 = Borderline conference\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Acceptance rate ~21%\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n**Core claims:**\n1. CSD pipeline (Counterfactual Style Decomposition) for per-axis style bias detection in black-box LLM judges\n2. \"Likert Blindspot\": pairwise comparison detects style bias in 16/18 cells while Likert detects only 2/18\n3. GEE-based confound gradient decomposition (verbosity=confound-free, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated)\n4. Practitioner guidance: use pairwise over Likert, apply per-axis confound decomposition\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- CSD Pipeline 5 stages: (1) Source text from LLMBar/AlpacaEval/MT-Bench, 100 pairs each; (2) Counterfactual generation via Qwen3-32B with axis-specific prompts; (3) NLI content verification (pass rates 82-94%); (4) Position-controlled judge scoring (both orderings A-B and B-A), collecting pairwise + Likert on same data; (5) BT model aggregation + paired t-test for Likert ATE + GEE decomposition\n- GEE model: logit(P(prefer_i)) = β0 + βs·Si + βo·Oi, where Si = preferred-style variant position, Oi = original text position. ORs = exp(βs), ORo = exp(βo).\n- Assumptions stated: (1) rewriting modifies only target style (NLI verified), (2) position control eliminates order effects, (3) BT model adequate. Causal DAG in supplementary.\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n\nTable 1 — Cross-judge formality (LLMBar, n=100):\n| Judge | BT | 95% CI | Likert p | Blind |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| GPT-4o | 0.630 | [.558,.703] | .332 | ✓ |\n| Qwen3-32B | 0.665 | [.585,.739] | .908 | ✓ |\n| Claude-3.5 | 0.645 | [.565,.725] | .641 | ✓ |\n| Gemini-2.0 | 0.680 | [.595,.755] | .782 | ✓ |\n\nTable 2 — Cross-benchmark formality (3 judges × 3 benchmarks):\nAll 9 cells significant (p<0.025), BT 0.590–0.680. Cochran's Q p=0.42 (no heterogeneity).\n\nTable 3 — Likert vs Pairwise detection across 18 cells:\n- Pairwise significant: 16/18\n- Likert significant: 2/18 (both verbosity with BT>0.75)\n- Blindspot rate: 87.5%, median sensitivity ratio: 4.8×\n\nTable 4 — 2×3 Blindspot matrix (GPT-4o + Qwen3-32B × formality/verbosity/register):\nAll 6 cells confirm Blindspot. Register cells are exploratory (BT marginally significant).\n\nTable 5 — Scaled replication n=100 vs n=400:\nBT stable (0.630→0.648), CI width shrinks (0.145→0.086), Likert ATE still NS (p=0.185), binarized Likert marginal (p=0.042) consistent with power curve prediction n≈462.\n\nTable 6 — GEE confound gradient:\n| Axis | GPT-4o ORs | GPT-4o ORo | Qwen ORs | Qwen ORo |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Verbosity (CF) | 10.5*** | 0.62 NS | 13.1*** | 0.49 NS |\n| Formality (MX) | 2.21** | 1.82† | 4.20*** | 4.70*** |\n| Register (OD) | 1.75† | 4.15*** | 1.91* | 5.72*** |\n\nTable 7 — RATE originality control (original vs double-rewrite, same style):\nOriginal preference BT 0.540-0.575 (all NS or marginal), consistently smaller than style BT 0.630-0.680.\n\nRewriter independence: Llama-3.1-70B rewriter gives comparable results (NLI 84% vs 88%, BT diff <0.03, p>0.40).\n\nRegister GEE cross-validation: GPT-4o replicates originality-dominated pattern of Qwen3-32B.\n\n**Limitations stated:** NLI false negatives possible, only 3 style axes, n=100-400 may miss very small biases, GEE assumes logit-linear.\n\nPaper is 6 pages main text (within 8-page limit). Has Limitations section. 10 references.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what was planned)\nThe experiment plan included these experiments:\n- exp-001: Multi-judge formality pairwise (Claude + Gemini) — COVERED in Table 1\n- exp-002: Multi-benchmark formality (AlpacaEval + MT-Bench) — COVERED in Table 2\n- exp-003: Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity analysis across all judges — COVERED in Table 3-4\n- exp-005: RATE control (original vs double-rewrite) — COVERED in Table 7\n- exp-006: Scaled replication n=400 — COVERED in Table 5\n- exp-008: GPT-4o register scoring + GEE — COVERED in Table 6 + Section 4.5\n- exp-009: Non-Qwen rewriter control — COVERED in Section 4.5\n- exp-020: GPT-4o verbosity Likert ATE + register GEE — COVERED in Table 4\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions. For each, give a score 1-5 and detailed justification:\n\n1. **Soundness** (1-5): Are the methods technically correct? Are statistical tests appropriate? Are conclusions supported by evidence? Look for: multiple comparisons correction, bootstrap details, GEE specification validity, NLI verification adequacy, potential confounds not addressed.\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor** (1-5): Coverage, controls, sample sizes, reproducibility. Are there missing experiments? Is the evidence sufficient for the claims?\n\n3. **Reproducibility** (1-5): Could someone replicate this? Are prompts, models, parameters, data sources specified?\n\n4. **Weaknesses** — List every methodological/experimental weakness you find, ordered by severity. Be specific about what's missing or flawed.\n\n5. **Strengths** — List methodological/experimental strengths.\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** — Specific questions you'd want answered in a rebuttal.\n\n7. **Preliminary Score Recommendation** (use the ARR scale above) with brief justification.\n\nBe rigorous but fair. EMNLP reviewers should flag real problems, not manufacture objections."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":30060,"cache_read_input_tokens":23877,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":30060},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3b839b6-f829-41eb-a911-4574b0a14ec9","uuid":"77698713-8ed3-4a05-9a72-e04120e380bd"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a2f783d37263bbb69","tool_use_id":"toolu_01FJcotXASHd7kdeVDZSpUAd","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Provide an independent, blind review. Do NOT speculate about how good/bad the paper is in advance — judge purely on the evidence.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3.5 = Borderline conference\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Acceptance rate ~21%\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n**Core claims:**\n1. CSD pipeline (Counterfactual Style Decomposition) for per-axis style bias detection in black-box LLM judges\n2. \"Likert Blindspot\": pairwise comparison detects style bias in 16/18 cells while Likert detects only 2/18\n3. GEE-based confound gradient decomposition (verbosity=confound-free, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated)\n4. Practitioner guidance: use pairwise over Likert, apply per-axis confound decomposition\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- CSD Pipeline 5 stages: (1) Source text from LLMBar/AlpacaEval/MT-Bench, 100 pairs each; (2) Counterfactual generation via Qwen3-32B with axis-specific prompts; (3) NLI content verification (pass rates 82-94%); (4) Position-controlled judge scoring (both orderings A-B and B-A), collecting pairwise + Likert on same data; (5) BT model aggregation + paired t-test for Likert ATE + GEE decomposition\n- GEE model: logit(P(prefer_i)) = β0 + βs·Si + βo·Oi, where Si = preferred-style variant position, Oi = original text position. ORs = exp(βs), ORo = exp(βo).\n- Assumptions stated: (1) rewriting modifies only target style (NLI verified), (2) position control eliminates order effects, (3) BT model adequate. Causal DAG in supplementary.\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n\nTable 1 — Cross-judge formality (LLMBar, n=100):\n| Judge | BT | 95% CI | Likert p | Blind |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| GPT-4o | 0.630 | [.558,.703] | .332 | ✓ |\n| Qwen3-32B | 0.665 | [.585,.739] | .908 | ✓ |\n| Claude-3.5 | 0.645 | [.565,.725] | .641 | ✓ |\n| Gemini-2.0 | 0.680 | [.595,.755] | .782 | ✓ |\n\nTable 2 — Cross-benchmark formality (3 judges × 3 benchmarks):\nAll 9 cells significant (p<0.025), BT 0.590–0.680. Cochran's Q p=0.42 (no heterogeneity).\n\nTable 3 — Likert vs Pairwise detection across 18 cells:\n- Pairwise significant: 16/18\n- Likert significant: 2/18 (both verbosity with BT>0.75)\n- Blindspot rate: 87.5%, median sensitivity ratio: 4.8×\n\nTable 4 — 2×3 Blindspot matrix (GPT-4o + Qwen3-32B × formality/verbosity/register):\nAll 6 cells confirm Blindspot. Register cells are exploratory (BT marginally significant).\n\nTable 5 — Scaled replication n=100 vs n=400:\nBT stable (0.630→0.648), CI width shrinks (0.145→0.086), Likert ATE still NS (p=0.185), binarized Likert marginal (p=0.042) consistent with power curve prediction n≈462.\n\nTable 6 — GEE confound gradient:\n| Axis | GPT-4o ORs | GPT-4o ORo | Qwen ORs | Qwen ORo |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Verbosity (CF) | 10.5*** | 0.62 NS | 13.1*** | 0.49 NS |\n| Formality (MX) | 2.21** | 1.82† | 4.20*** | 4.70*** |\n| Register (OD) | 1.75† | 4.15*** | 1.91* | 5.72*** |\n\nTable 7 — RATE originality control (original vs double-rewrite, same style):\nOriginal preference BT 0.540-0.575 (all NS or marginal), consistently smaller than style BT 0.630-0.680.\n\nRewriter independence: Llama-3.1-70B rewriter gives comparable results (NLI 84% vs 88%, BT diff <0.03, p>0.40).\n\nRegister GEE cross-validation: GPT-4o replicates originality-dominated pattern of Qwen3-32B.\n\n**Limitations stated:** NLI false negatives possible, only 3 style axes, n=100-400 may miss very small biases, GEE assumes logit-linear.\n\nPaper is 6 pages main text (within 8-page limit). Has Limitations section. 10 references.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what was planned)\nThe experiment plan included these experiments:\n- exp-001: Multi-judge formality pairwise (Claude + Gemini) — COVERED in Table 1\n- exp-002: Multi-benchmark formality (AlpacaEval + MT-Bench) — COVERED in Table 2\n- exp-003: Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity analysis across all judges — COVERED in Table 3-4\n- exp-005: RATE control (original vs double-rewrite) — COVERED in Table 7\n- exp-006: Scaled replication n=400 — COVERED in Table 5\n- exp-008: GPT-4o register scoring + GEE — COVERED in Table 6 + Section 4.5\n- exp-009: Non-Qwen rewriter control — COVERED in Section 4.5\n- exp-020: GPT-4o verbosity Likert ATE + register GEE — COVERED in Table 4\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions. For each, give a score 1-5 and detailed justification:\n\n1. **Soundness** (1-5): Are the methods technically correct? Are statistical tests appropriate? Are conclusions supported by evidence? Look for: multiple comparisons correction, bootstrap details, GEE specification validity, NLI verification adequacy, potential confounds not addressed.\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor** (1-5): Coverage, controls, sample sizes, reproducibility. Are there missing experiments? Is the evidence sufficient for the claims?\n\n3. **Reproducibility** (1-5): Could someone replicate this? Are prompts, models, parameters, data sources specified?\n\n4. **Weaknesses** — List every methodological/experimental weakness you find, ordered by severity. Be specific about what's missing or flawed.\n\n5. **Strengths** — List methodological/experimental strengths.\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** — Specific questions you'd want answered in a rebuttal.\n\n7. **Preliminary Score Recommendation** (use the ARR scale above) with brief justification.\n\nBe rigorous but fair. EMNLP reviewers should flag real problems, not manufacture objections.","uuid":"771218b0-3a69-497f-971f-abda2dfd4532","session_id":"e3b839b6-f829-41eb-a911-4574b0a14ec9"}
