{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016D9pJsK8iGjQE5DBXztFZ8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\n2\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n3\t\n4\t\n5\t\\subsection{Statistical Framework}\n6\t\\label{sec:stat_framework}\n7\t\n8\tWe designate a primary family of nine hypothesis tests (three judges $\\times$ three style axes) and control the family-wise error rate with Holm-Bonferroni correction at $\\alpha = 0.05$.\n9\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} reports both raw and adjusted $p$-values for this $3 \\times 3$ grid.\n10\tAnalyses outside this grid are classified as either \\emph{exploratory} (NLI threshold sensitivity sweep, \\S\\ref{sec:nli_threshold}) or \\emph{confirmatory} (consistency filter validation, \\S\\ref{sec:consistency}).\n11\tExploratory analyses are reported with uncorrected $p$-values and interpreted as hypothesis-generating; confirmatory analyses test specific hypotheses about pipeline validity and are evaluated at the nominal $\\alpha = 0.05$ level without additional correction, as they address orthogonal questions about measurement reliability rather than the primary style-bias family.\n12\t\n13\t\\begin{table}[t]\n14\t\\centering\n15\t\\small\n16\t\\caption{Main results across two judges and three style axes. BT: Bradley-Terry win probability (pair-level bootstrap). $\\dagger$: Likert Blindspot (pairwise significant, Likert non-significant). All six cells show the Blindspot pattern.}\n17\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n18\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n19\t\\toprule\n20\tJudge & Axis & BT $P$ {[95\\% CI]} & Likert $d$ ($p$) & \\\\\n21\t\\midrule\n22\tGPT-4o & Formality & .630 {[.558, .703]}$^{***}$ & .011 (.908) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n23\tGPT-4o & Verbosity & .760 {[.667, .853]}$^{***}$ & $-$.095 (.403) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n24\tGPT-4o & Register & .570 {[.468, .673]}$^{*}$ & $-$.031 (.785) & $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$ \\\\\n25\t\\midrule\n26\tQwen3-32B & Formality & .665 {[.585, .739]}$^{***}$ & .011 (.908) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n27\tQwen3-32B & Verbosity & .773 {[.703, .843]}$^{***}$ & $-$.151 (.179) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n28\tQwen3-32B & Register & .593$^{\\text{ST}}$ ($p = .220$) & (.505) & $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$ \\\\\n29\t\\bottomrule\n30\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\footnotesize $^{*}p < 0.05$; $^{***}p < 0.001$.\\quad $^{\\text{ST}}$Sign test (BT pending).\\quad $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$Exploratory (\\S\\ref{sec:negative_control}).}\n31\t\\end{tabular}\n32\t\\end{table}\n33\t\n34\t\n35\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n36\t\\centering\n37\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/forest_plot.pdf}\n38\t\\caption{Bradley-Terry win probabilities across two judges and three style axes. Filled diamonds: significant ($p < 0.05$); open diamonds: non-significant. Error bars: 95\\% bootstrap CI. The dashed line at 0.50 indicates no style preference. Verbosity shows the strongest and most consistent bias across both judges.}\n39\t\\label{fig:forest_plot}\n40\t\\end{figure}\n41\t\n42\t\\section{Analysis}\n43\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n44\t\n45\t\n46\t\\subsection{NLI Gate Validation}\n47\t\\label{sec:nli_validation}\n48\t\n49\t\\paragraph{False negative rate.}\n50\tA potential concern is that the NLI entailment gate (DeBERTa-v3-large, threshold 0.90) rejects too many content-preserving pairs.\n51\tTo quantify this, we validate all 100 NLI decisions against an independent LLM annotator (Qwen3-32B) prompted to judge whether semantic content is preserved.\n52\tThe two systems agree on 91 of 100 pairs (Gwet's AC1 $= 0.89$; Cohen's $\\kappa = 0.43$ is depressed by prevalence skew, a well-documented paradox \\citep{feinstein1990kappa}).\n53\tOf the 12 pairs rejected by the NLI gate, 8 are judged content-preserving by the LLM annotator.\n54\tThe overall false negative rate is therefore $8/95 = 8.4\\%$, where the denominator counts all pairs that \\emph{any} annotator considers content-preserving.\n55\tThe figure sometimes cited as ``67\\%'' is the within-rejected rate ($8/12$), which conflates base-rate information with error severity.\n56\t\n57\tThree additional analyses confirm that these false negatives do not compromise downstream conclusions.\n58\tFirst, an NLI threshold sensitivity sweep (exp-011; \\S\\ref{sec:nli_threshold}) shows that the formality win rate remains 72.3--73.9\\% across thresholds from 0.70 to 0.95, with all sign test $p < 0.005$.\n59\tRelaxing the gate to admit these borderline pairs does not change the result.\n60\tSecond, the consistency filter analysis (exp-012; \\S\\ref{sec:consistency}) finds no systematic difference between retained and discarded pairs across four covariates (all Mann-Whitney $p > 0.12$).\n61\tThird, the 8 false negatives split 6:2 between formal-to-casual and casual-to-formal directions (75\\%:25\\%), and a Fisher exact test yields $p = 0.35$, ruling out directional bias in what the gate filters out.\n62\t\n63\tQualitative inspection of the 8 false negatives reveals that 3--4 cases involve prompt leakage or subtle content additions that the LLM annotator tolerates but the NLI gate correctly flags (detailed in supplementary materials).\n64\tThe NLI gate operates as a precision-oriented conservative filter: it sacrifices a bounded amount of recall (8.4\\% false negative rate) to ensure near-zero contamination of the downstream analysis (precision $= 98.9\\%$, i.e., only 1 of 88 NLI-passed pairs is flagged by the LLM as content-altered).\n65\t\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Threshold sensitivity.}\n68\t\\label{sec:nli_threshold}\n69\tThe formality win rate is stable at 72.3--73.9\\% across NLI thresholds of 0.70, 0.90, and 0.95 (sign test $p < 0.005$ at every threshold).\n70\tNLI scores follow a bimodal distribution (most pairs above 0.95 or below 0.30), so the 0.90 threshold lies on a plateau rather than a cliff edge.\n71\t\n72\t\n73\t\\subsection{Measurement Sensitivity: Likert vs.\\ Pairwise}\n74\t\\label{sec:likert_pairwise}\n75\t\n76\t\\paragraph{Head-to-head comparison with RATE.}\n77\tApplying RATE's Likert ATE methodology \\citep{reber2025rate} to our formality style-transformed pairs yields Cohen's $d = 0.10$, $p = 0.33$ (non-significant, $n = 88$); pairwise comparison on identical data reveals a 63.0\\% formal preference ($p < 0.001$, Bradley-Terry on all 173 comparisons), confirmed by a consistency-filtered sign test (73.9\\%, $p = 0.006$, $n = 46$ consistent pairs).\n78\tThis is the Likert Blindspot: the same data, the same style intervention, two measurement instruments, opposite conclusions about whether formality bias exists.\n79\t\n80\tThe two frameworks are complementary rather than contradictory.\n81\tRATE measures aggregate attribute-level quality effects via Likert ATE and is designed to detect mean score shifts on continuous rating scales.\n82\tCSD targets isolated per-axis style preference via forced-choice pairwise comparison, which eliminates ties and is more sensitive to ordinal preferences that do not manifest as mean differences.\n83\tThe Likert Blindspot arises because Likert tie rates cluster at 58--73\\% across all six judge--axis configurations (e.g., GPT-4o formality 62.5\\%, Qwen3-32B verbosity 58.1\\%)---a structural property of integer scales, not a judge-specific artifact, that collapses real preference signal into uninformative ties before any statistical test is applied.\n84\t\n85\t\n86\t\\paragraph{Bradley-Terry analysis.}\n87\tAs the primary pairwise analysis, we compute a regularized estimate of the formal win probability across all 173 valid comparisons (88 NLI-gated pairs $\\times$ 2 position trials, excluding 3 invalid trials), including position-inconsistent pairs that the consistency filter discards, using a Bradley-Terry model \\citep{bradley1952rank} with pair-level bootstrap confidence intervals.\n88\tThe estimated formal win probability is 63.0\\% (bootstrap 95\\% CI: $[55.8\\%, 70.3\\%]$, $p < 0.001$, 2{,}000 resamples); both CI bounds exceed the 50\\% null, and the interval width (14.5pp) reflects the moderate sample size.\n89\tFor comparison, the consistency-filtered sign test yields 73.9\\% (34/46 consistent pairs, $p = 0.006$).\n90\tThat the full-data estimate (63.0\\%) is \\emph{lower} than the filtered estimate (73.9\\%) rules out the concern that the consistency filter inflates the effect.\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{Sensitivity hierarchy across measurement methods.}\n93\tTo confirm that the Likert Blindspot is not an artifact of a particular statistical test, we apply seven measurement methods to the same formality data and rank them by sensitivity (Table~\\ref{tab:sensitivity}).\n94\tBoth pairwise methods detect the formality preference; all Likert-based methods fail regardless of whether they assume interval scaling (ATE $t$-test), ordinal scaling (ordinal logistic regression), or nonparametric ranking (Wilcoxon signed-rank).\n95\t\n96\t\\begin{table}[t]\n97\t\\centering\n98\t\\small\n99\t\\caption{Sensitivity hierarchy: seven measurement methods applied to the same formality style-transformed data. Only pairwise methods (top two rows) reach significance. All Likert-based methods fail.}\n100\t\\label{tab:sensitivity}\n101\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n102\t\\toprule\n103\tMethod & Statistic & $p$ & Data \\\\\n104\t\\midrule\n105\tBT model & 63.0\\% $[55.8, 70.3]$ & ${<}0.001$ & All 173 comp. \\\\\n106\tSign test (filtered) & 73.9\\% (34/46) & 0.006 & Consistent \\\\\n107\t\\midrule\n108\tLikert ATE ($t$-test) & $d = 0.102$ & 0.332 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n109\tBinarized Likert & 60.6\\% & 0.296 & Non-tied \\\\\n110\tOrdinal reg.\\ (prop.\\ odds) & $\\beta = {-}0.079$ & 0.769 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n111\tGEE (exchangeable) & $\\beta = {-}0.084$ & 0.587 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n112\tWilcoxon signed-rank & $W = 274$ & 0.905 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n113\t\\bottomrule\n114\t\\end{tabular}\n115\t\\end{table}\n116\t\n117\tOrdinal logistic regression ($n = 88$ pairs, 176 observations) correctly respects the ordinal nature of Likert data.\n118\tWithout pair random effects, the ordered model is anti-conservative (favors detection), yet the formality coefficient remains non-significant ($\\beta = -0.079$, 95\\% CI $[-0.60, 0.45]$, $p = 0.769$); GEE with pair-level clustering confirms ($p = 0.587$).\n119\tThis rules out the possibility that the Blindspot is an artifact of interval-scale assumptions---Likert scores are indistinguishable from chance regardless of statistical approach ($p \\geq 0.33$), while pairwise comparison detects a clear preference ($p = 0.006$).\n120\tTo illustrate the sensitivity gap between measurement approaches, Figure~\\ref{fig:power_curve} plots detection probability curves at effect magnitudes matching our observed data.\n121\tThis is not a post-hoc power analysis (cf.\\ \\citealt{hoenig2001abuse}), but a visual comparison of how measurement structure affects the sample size needed to detect effects of a given magnitude.\n122\t\n123\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n124\t\\centering\n125\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/power_curve.pdf}\n126\t\\caption{Detection probability as a function of sample size for four measurement approaches at observed effect magnitudes. 80\\% power crossings: sign test $n \\approx 62$, BT $n \\approx 114$, binarized Likert $n \\approx 462$, Likert ATE $n \\approx 1{,}473$. At typical evaluation scales ($n = 50$--$200$, shaded), both pairwise methods reach 80\\% power while both Likert-based methods remain far below---the practical consequence of the Likert Blindspot. The ATE curve uses the raw (uncorrected) Cohen's $d = 0.073$ rather than the RATE-corrected $d = 0.104$, providing a conservative estimate that does not depend on rewrite-bias correction assumptions.}\n127\t\\label{fig:power_curve}\n128\t\\end{figure}\n129\t\n130\t\\subsection{Cross-Judge Replication: Qwen3-32B}\n131\t\\label{sec:cross_judge}\n132\t\n133\tTo test whether the Likert Blindspot generalizes beyond a single judge, we replicate the formality analysis with Qwen3-32B, an open-source model architecturally distinct from GPT-4o.\n134\tBradley-Terry analysis yields a formal win probability of 66.5\\% (bootstrap 95\\% CI: $[58.5\\%, 73.9\\%]$, $p < 0.0001$, 10{,}000 resamples), while Likert ATE on the same data is non-significant ($d = 0.011$, $p = 0.908$).\n135\tThe Blindspot replicates: pairwise comparison detects a clear formality bias that Likert scoring misses entirely.\n136\tThe higher BT estimate (66.5\\% vs.\\ 63.0\\% for GPT-4o) may partly reflect evaluator-producer bias, as Qwen3-32B also serves as the rewriter (\\S\\ref{sec:limitations}).\n137\t\n138\t\\subsection{Cross-Axis Replication: Verbosity}\n139\t\\label{sec:cross_axis}\n140\t\n141\tThe verbosity axis provides the strongest and cleanest demonstration of the Blindspot.\n142\tBoth judges show near-identical verbosity preference: GPT-4o 76.0\\% (BT, bootstrap 95\\% CI: $[66.7\\%, 85.3\\%]$, $p = 0.0001$) and Qwen3-32B 77.3\\% ($[70.3\\%, 84.3\\%]$, $p < 0.0001$), the largest effects observed in our experiments, while Likert ATE remains non-significant for both ($d = -0.095$, $p = 0.403$; $d = -0.151$, $p = 0.179$).\n143\tUnlike formality, where the style effect is confounded with originality, verbosity exhibits a confound-free style bias for both judges: GEE decomposition confirms massive style effects (Qwen3-32B $\\text{OR} = 13.10$; GPT-4o $\\text{OR} = 10.97$; both $p < .0001$) while the originality component is non-significant in both (Qwen3-32B $\\text{OR} = 0.49$, $p = .096$; GPT-4o $\\text{OR} = 0.44$, n.s.; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n144\tBoth rewriting directions favor verbose responses, with the concise$\\to$verbose direction (90.7\\%) even exceeding verbose$\\to$concise (80.6\\%), providing convergent directional and GEE evidence that the full 77.3\\% effect is attributable to style rather than originality.\n145\t\n146\t\\subsection{Register: Weak Evidence with Strong Originality Confound}\n147\t\\label{sec:negative_control}\n148\t\n149\tRegister (academic vs.\\ conversational tone) provides weak but directionally consistent evidence for a third axis across both judges.\n150\tGPT-4o yields a marginally significant BT estimate (57.0\\%, $[46.8\\%, 67.3\\%]$, $p = 0.04$); Qwen3-32B's raw sign test is non-significant (59.3\\%, $p = 0.220$).\n151\tLikert ATE is non-significant for both judges ($p = 0.785$; $p = 0.505$).\n152\tHowever, direction analysis reveals that both rewriting directions favor academic text: academic$\\to$conversational pairs show 89.3\\% academic preference ($p < 0.0001$), while conversational$\\to$academic pairs show 68.8\\% academic preference ($p = 0.050$).\n153\tGEE logistic decomposition separates the style and originality components: the register effect is marginally significant after controlling for originality ($\\text{OR} = 1.91$, 95\\% CI $[1.01, 3.58]$, $p = 0.046$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}), but the confidence interval lower bound approaches unity and the originality confound is the strongest observed across all axes ($\\text{OR} = 5.72$, $p < 0.0001$).\n154\tThis finding is classified as exploratory and does not enter the primary Holm-Bonferroni family.\n155\t\n156\tTogether with tone---which fails structurally at the NLI gate (65\\% pass rate, below the 86\\% achieved by direction-aware verification for verbosity)---register illustrates the axis-dependent nature of CSD: the framework correctly identifies axes where evidence is weak or where the pipeline itself cannot produce valid counterfactuals.\n157\t\n158\t\\subsection{Consistency Filter Robustness}\n159\t\\label{sec:consistency}\n160\t\n161\tThe position-consistency filter retains 55 of 98 valid pairs (56.1\\%), discarding those where the judge's preference reverses across position permutations.\n162\tTo address the concern that discarding 43.9\\% of pairs introduces selection bias, we compare consistent and inconsistent groups across four covariates and find no significant differences (all Mann-Whitney $p > 0.12$):\n163\tNLI entailment score ($p = 0.824$), BERTScore ($p = 0.12$), original text length ($p = 0.137$), and rewrite length difference ($p = 0.715$).\n164\tThe two groups are statistically indistinguishable on every measured dimension, indicating that the consistency filter selects on judge decisiveness rather than on text properties that could confound the style-bias estimate.\n165\t\n166\tThe Bradley-Terry analysis (\\S\\ref{sec:likert_pairwise}) confirms this: the BT model uses all 173 comparisons including position-inconsistent pairs and yields a formal win probability of 63.0\\% ($p < 0.001$), consistent with the filtered sign test (73.9\\%, $p = 0.006$).\n167\tThe agreement between the two methods---one discarding noisy pairs, the other retaining and downweighting them---provides strong evidence that the consistency filter does not introduce systematic bias.\n168\t\n169\t\\subsection{GEE Decomposition and Confound Structure}\n170\t\\label{sec:direction}\n171\t\n172\tDirection analysis reveals partial confounding between style and original quality across axes; GEE logistic decomposition disentangles the two effects (full direction-asymmetry analysis in Appendix~\\ref{app:direction}).\n173\tAfter controlling for original preference, the formality effect remains significant for both judges (GPT-4o: $\\text{OR} = 2.21$, $p = 0.010$; Qwen3-32B: $\\text{OR} = 4.20$, $p = 0.0002$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}), with the cross-judge magnitude gap consistent with evaluator-producer bias (\\S\\ref{sec:limitations}).\n174\t\n175\t\\begin{table}[t]\n176\t\\centering\n177\t\\small\n178\t\\caption{GEE logistic decomposition (exchangeable correlation) into style and originality effects, ordered by confound structure. Verbosity is confound-free; register is originality-dominated.}\n179\t\\label{tab:gee}\n180\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n181\t\\toprule\n182\t & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Style OR} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Originality OR} \\\\\n183\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n184\tConfig & OR [95\\% CI] & $p$ & OR [95\\% CI] & $p$ \\\\\n185\t\\midrule\n186\tQwen3 verb.\\ & 13.10 [5.62, 30.55] & ${<}.0001$ & 0.49 [0.21, 1.13] & .096 \\\\\n187\tGPT-4o verb.\\ & 10.97 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.44 & n.s. \\\\\n188\t\\midrule\n189\tQwen3 form.\\ & 4.20 [1.99, 8.87] & .0002 & 4.70 [2.22, 9.96] & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n190\tGPT-4o form.\\ & 2.21 [1.21, 4.04] & .010 & 1.82 [0.99, 3.32] & .052 \\\\\n191\t\\midrule\n192\tGPT-4o reg.\\ & 1.88 & .029 & 2.21 & .006 \\\\\n193\tQwen3 reg.\\ & 1.91 [1.01, 3.58] & .046 & 5.72 [3.02, 10.82] & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n194\t\\bottomrule\n195\t\\end{tabular}\n196\t\\end{table}\n197\t\n198\tThe confound gradient replicates across both judges: verbosity exhibits pure style bias (Style OR $= 13.10$ / $10.97$, Originality OR $= 0.49$ / $0.44$, both n.s.), formality shows mixed style and originality effects, while register is originality-confounded (Originality OR $= 5.72$ / $2.21$, both $p < 0.01$).\n199\tThis cross-judge replication of the gradient---not just the direction but the qualitative confound structure---demonstrates that per-axis decomposition captures genuine axis-level properties rather than judge-specific artifacts.\n200\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FJcotXASHd7kdeVDZSpUAd","session_id":"e3b839b6-f829-41eb-a911-4574b0a14ec9","uuid":"ea1c88b5-b16f-48ec-a4f4-c7f784c37b33","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:53:33.300Z"}
