{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDf7211PPtrVHaGNvqQ4LS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,twocolumn]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n13\t\\usepackage{times}\n14\t\n15\t\\setlength{\\columnsep}{0.6cm}\n16\t\\captionsetup{font=small,labelfont=bf}\n17\t\n18\t\\newcommand{\\bt}{\\textsc{bt}}\n19\t\\newcommand{\\cmark}{\\checkmark}\n20\t\n21\t\\title{The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison}\n22\t\n23\t\\author{Anonymous}\n24\t\\date{}\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{document}\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\t% ABSTRACT\n31\t% ============================================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tLarge Language Model (LLM) judges are widely used for evaluating text quality, yet their susceptibility to surface-level style features remains poorly characterized.\n34\tWe introduce the \\textbf{Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD)} pipeline, which generates style-only counterfactual pairs via constrained rewriting, verifies content preservation through Natural Language Inference (NLI), and measures per-axis style bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n35\tApplying CSD to four LLM judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Gemini-2.0-Flash) across three style axes (formality, verbosity, register) and three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), we identify a systematic \\textbf{Likert Blindspot}: pairwise comparison detects significant style bias in 16 of 18 judge$\\times$axis$\\times$benchmark cells (Bradley-Terry win probabilities 0.59--0.86, all $p<0.05$), while standard Likert scoring remains non-significant in all but 2 cells.\n36\tGeneralized Estimating Equation (GEE) decomposition reveals a \\textbf{confound gradient} across axes: verbosity bias is confound-free (style OR$=$10.5--13.1, originality NS), formality is mixed (both ORs 2--5), and register is originality-dominated (originality OR$=$4.2--5.7).\n37\tThis gradient replicates across judges and constitutes actionable guidance for practitioners: axis selection and confound decomposition are necessary steps when auditing LLM judge bias.\n38\t\\end{abstract}\n39\t\n40\t% ============================================================\n41\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n42\t% ============================================================\n43\t\\section{Introduction}\n44\t\n45\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become central infrastructure for both NLP benchmarking and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) alignment training.\n46\tRecent work demonstrates that surface-level style features, particularly response length, can dominate judge preferences: SOS-Bench reports a correlation of $R=0.999$ between style scores and overall judge ratings \\citep{sosbench2025}.\n47\tIf judges systematically prefer certain stylistic presentations independent of content quality, downstream model selection and alignment training inherit these biases.\n48\t\n49\tStandard practice evaluates LLM judge reliability using Likert-scale scoring, where judges assign absolute quality ratings.\n50\tHowever, the sensitivity of Likert scoring to style confounds has not been systematically examined.\n51\tWhen style effects are moderate in magnitude, high tie rates in absolute scoring (typically 55--65\\%) may render Likert evaluation effectively blind to real biases.\n52\tThis measurement gap is consequential: if standard evaluation methodology cannot detect style bias, practitioners have no basis for corrective action.\n53\t\n54\tWe propose a three-stage Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD) pipeline that addresses this gap.\n55\tFirst, we generate binary style counterfactuals through constrained LLM rewriting (e.g., transforming formal text to casual while preserving content).\n56\tSecond, we verify content preservation via NLI filtering (pass rate 82--94\\% across axes).\n57\tThird, we measure judge preference using position-controlled pairwise comparison and aggregate via Bradley-Terry (BT) models, which capture preference signal that Likert scoring misses.\n58\tWe further introduce GEE decomposition to separate style effects from originality confounds at the pair level.\n59\t\n60\tOur experiments across 4 judges, 3 style axes, and 3 benchmarks reveal two main findings.\n61\tThe \\textit{Likert Blindspot}: in 16/18 tested cells, pairwise comparison detects significant style bias while Likert scoring does not, even at 4$\\times$ sample size.\n62\tThe \\textit{confound gradient}: GEE decomposition shows that the confound structure of style bias varies systematically across axes, from confound-free (verbosity) to originality-dominated (register), and this pattern replicates across judges.\n63\t\n64\tOur contributions are:\n65\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n66\t\\item The CSD pipeline for post-hoc, per-axis style bias detection in black-box LLM judges, validated across 4 judges $\\times$ 3 axes $\\times$ 3 benchmarks (\\S\\ref{sec:method}, Tables~\\ref{tab:crossjudge}--\\ref{tab:crossbench}).\n67\t\\item The Likert Blindspot: systematic evidence that standard Likert scoring masks style biases detectable by pairwise comparison, with a median sensitivity ratio of 4.8$\\times$ (\\S\\ref{sec:blindspot}, Table~\\ref{tab:blindspot}).\n68\t\\item GEE-based confound gradient decomposition, revealing axis-specific confound structure that replicates across judges (\\S\\ref{sec:gradient}, Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n69\t\\item Practitioner guidance: use pairwise over Likert for style bias audits, and apply per-axis confound decomposition before interpreting results (\\S\\ref{sec:discussion}).\n70\t\\end{enumerate}\n71\t\n72\t% ============================================================\n73\t% 2. RELATED WORK\n74\t% ============================================================\n75\t\\section{Related Work}\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{LLM Judge Bias Detection.}\n78\tSeveral recent studies examine biases in LLM-as-judge evaluation.\n79\t\\citet{lcalpacaeval2025} address length bias through regression-based length control in AlpacaEval, demonstrating that verbosity alone can inflate win rates.\n80\tSOS-Bench \\citep{sosbench2025} quantifies judge sensitivity to injected quality defects and finds that style features dominate overall ratings ($R=0.999$).\n81\tCASSE \\citep{casse2026} constructs a 12$\\times$12 style perturbation matrix but does not separate causal contributions of individual style features from their correlations.\n82\tThese approaches establish that style bias exists but do not provide per-axis measurement with controlled confound decomposition.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Counterfactual Evaluation Methods.}\n85\tRATE \\citep{rate2025} proposes a double-rewrite protocol for measuring treatment effects on judge scores, using Likert-based average treatment effect (ATE) as the primary metric.\n86\tOur work demonstrates that this Likert-based measurement systematically underestimates style effects (Table~\\ref{tab:blindspot}), suggesting that RATE's own findings may be affected by the Blindspot.\n87\t\\citet{counterfactual_rhetorical2025} use persona-level counterfactuals with BT aggregation, but at a coarser granularity than per-axis decomposition.\n88\t\n89\t\\paragraph{Pairwise vs.\\ Likert Measurement.}\n90\tThe psychometric literature has long recognized that forced-choice comparisons yield higher sensitivity than absolute ratings for detecting small effect sizes \\citep{decoster2011,stevens1946,feinstein1990}.\n91\tBT models \\citep{bradleyterry1952} provide a principled framework for aggregating pairwise preferences.\n92\tIn the LLM evaluation context, \\citet{zeng2024} advocate BT-based aggregation for judge benchmarking but do not examine the Likert sensitivity gap for style bias specifically.\n93\tOur contribution is the first systematic, multi-judge, multi-axis quantification of this sensitivity gap in the style bias domain.\n94\t\n95\t\\paragraph{Style and Originality Confounds.}\n96\tPreference for original over rewritten text (``originality bias'') is a known confound in counterfactual studies.\n97\tGEE models \\citep{liang1986} with exchangeable correlation structure enable joint estimation of style and originality effects at the pair level, decomposing the overall preference signal.\n98\tWe show that this decomposition reveals a structured gradient across style axes, from confound-free to originality-dominated.\n99\t\n100\t% ============================================================"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FJcotXASHd7kdeVDZSpUAd","session_id":"e3b839b6-f829-41eb-a911-4574b0a14ec9","uuid":"04c70418-87cf-472c-80a6-2ba5a644a9fe","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:54:05.548Z"}
