{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q5sjTDUxN1hdqsBFcLEUmQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related_work}\n3\t\n4\tTwo lines of work motivate our approach: counterfactual methods for diagnosing judge bias, and measurement theory on the sensitivity of different elicitation formats.\n5\t\n6\t\\paragraph{Counterfactual Approaches to Judge Bias}\n7\t\\citet{reber2025rate} propose RATE, a framework that rewrites model outputs along style attributes using imperfect counterfactuals and measures the causal effect on reward model scores via Likert-scale Average Treatment Effect (ATE).\n8\tRATE applies a correction term to account for rewriting imperfections, but its reliance on Likert ATE as the primary metric is a vulnerability: our experiments show that Likert scoring exhibits a ceiling effect in which 60\\% of style-transformed pairs receive identical integer scores, yielding non-significant ATE estimates ($p = 0.332$) for the same interventions that pairwise comparison detects at $p = 0.006$.\n9\tIf this Likert Blindspot generalizes beyond our setting, RATE's own style-related findings may underestimate the true magnitude of attribute effects.\n10\tCSD differs from RATE in three respects: it targets individual style axes rather than general attributes, it measures bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison rather than Likert ATE, and it enforces content preservation via NLI entailment gating rather than post-hoc correction for imperfect rewrites.\n11\t\\citet{counterfactual2025rhetorical} also adopt a counterfactual LLM-based design, rewriting texts to measure rhetorical style effects and aggregating preferences with a Bradley-Terry model.\n12\tTheir framework operates at persona-level granularity (e.g., ``writes like a professor''), whereas CSD intervenes on specific style axes (e.g., formality, conciseness) and uses a sign test with position control rather than parametric aggregation.\n13\t\n14\t\\paragraph{Measurement Sensitivity in NLG Evaluation}\n15\tThe distinction between ordinal and interval measurement dates to \\citet{stevens1946theory}, whose taxonomy of scales remains foundational.\n16\tThe superiority of pairwise comparison over rating scales for eliciting reliable human judgments has been established since \\citet{thurstone1927law}, whose Law of Comparative Judgment formalizes why forced-choice comparisons yield finer discriminability than absolute ratings.\n17\tMore broadly, \\citet{decoster2011median} show that artificial categorization of continuous variables---including the binarization step inherent in converting Likert scores to preference decisions---discards variance and reduces statistical power, a finding directly relevant to the Likert Blindspot mechanism we identify.\n18\tWithin NLP, \\citet{kiritchenko2017bws} demonstrate that best-worst scaling produces more reliable sentiment intensity annotations than Likert scales, and \\citet{novikova2018rankme} show that ranking-based evaluation detects quality differences in natural language generation systems that rating-based methods miss entirely.\n19\tOur contribution is not the discovery of this principle but the first systematic quantification of its magnitude for LLM judge style bias detection, along with the binarized Likert diagnostic that isolates the mechanism (measurement granularity, not test selection).\n20\t\n21\t\\paragraph{Style Bias Detection and Mitigation}\n22\tThe foundational LLM-as-judge framework was established by \\citet{zheng2023judging}, who introduced MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena and documented position bias in pairwise evaluation.\n23\t\\citet{wang2023faireval} further showed that preference rankings can be manipulated by swapping option order, proposing a win/tie/lose annotation scheme.\n24\t\\citet{li2024calibraeval} address selection bias through calibration of prediction distributions during inference.\n25\tThese works focus on \\emph{position} bias and calibration; style bias as a separate, dominant bias source has received attention only recently \\citep{murugadoss2025sos,li2026preference,soumik2026judging}.\n26\tMulti-feature frameworks such as CASSE \\citep{casse2026concise} and FairJudge \\citep{wang2026fairjudge} decompose style into finer dimensions but rely on correlational analyses without interventional controls.\n27\t\n28\t\\paragraph{Bradley-Terry Models in Evaluation}\n29\tThe Bradley-Terry model \\citep{bradley1952rank} underpins modern LLM evaluation platforms including Chatbot Arena \\citep{zheng2023judging} and AlpacaFarm \\citep{dubois2023alpacafarm}.\n30\tWe adopt BT as our primary pairwise analysis because it uses all comparison data---including position-inconsistent pairs---without requiring a consistency filter, yielding proper effect-size confidence intervals that sign tests cannot provide.\n31\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01L9BaE6xvP2ibtHum5jAjyH","session_id":"e3b839b6-f829-41eb-a911-4574b0a14ec9","uuid":"a561b5b2-c591-4b5b-b0ab-a754ed833bda","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:54:09.487Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QiRuBeSGE7YSiCshbAHpJH","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t% ============================================================\n101\t% 3. METHOD\n102\t% ============================================================\n103\t\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n104\t\n105\t\\subsection{CSD Pipeline Overview}\n106\t\n107\tThe Counterfactual Style Decomposition pipeline consists of five stages:\n108\t\n109\t\\textbf{Stage 1: Source Text Selection.}\n110\tWe sample evaluation instances from existing benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench).\n111\tFor each benchmark, we select 100 text pairs as source material.\n112\t\n113\t\\textbf{Stage 2: Counterfactual Generation.}\n114\tFor each source text and target style axis (formality, verbosity, register), we generate a style-transformed variant using an instruction-tuned LLM (Qwen3-32B) with axis-specific prompts.\n115\tThe rewriting prompt constrains the model to modify only the target style dimension while preserving semantic content, factual claims, and argument structure.\n116\t\n117\t\\textbf{Stage 3: NLI-Based Content Verification.}\n118\tEach original-counterfactual pair is verified for content preservation using a cross-encoder NLI model.\n119\tPairs where the NLI model detects entailment failure (content drift) are excluded.\n120\tPass rates range from 82\\% (MT-Bench) to 94\\% (register), with formality at 88\\% on LLMBar.\n121\t\n122\t\\textbf{Stage 4: Position-Controlled Judge Scoring.}\n123\tEach verified pair is presented to the target LLM judge in both orderings (A-B and B-A), yielding two preference judgments per pair.\n124\tWe collect both pairwise forced-choice preferences and Likert absolute quality ratings on the same counterfactual data.\n125\t\n126\t\\textbf{Stage 5: Statistical Analysis.}\n127\tPairwise preferences are aggregated via BT models with pair-level bootstrap confidence intervals.\n128\tLikert scores are analyzed via paired $t$-tests for ATE.\n129\tGEE models with logit link and exchangeable correlation decompose style and originality effects.\n130\t\n131\t\\subsection{GEE Confound Decomposition}\\label{sec:gee}\n132\t\n133\tFor each judge$\\times$axis cell, we fit a GEE model:\n134\t\\begin{equation}\n135\t\\text{logit}(P(\\text{prefer}_i)) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_s \\cdot S_i + \\beta_o \\cdot O_i\n136\t\\end{equation}\n137\twhere $S_i \\in \\{0,1\\}$ indicates whether the preferred-style variant is in position $i$, and $O_i \\in \\{0,1\\}$ indicates whether the original (vs.\\ rewritten) text is in position $i$.\n138\tThe odds ratios $\\text{OR}_s = e^{\\beta_s}$ and $\\text{OR}_o = e^{\\beta_o}$ quantify the independent contributions of style preference and originality preference.\n139\t\n140\tThis decomposition classifies each axis into one of three categories:\n141\t\\textit{confound-free} ($\\text{OR}_s$ significant, $\\text{OR}_o$ NS),\n142\t\\textit{mixed} (both significant), or\n143\t\\textit{originality-dominated} ($\\text{OR}_o \\gg \\text{OR}_s$).\n144\t\n145\t\\subsection{Assumptions and Limitations}\n146\t\n147\tOur interventional approach relies on three assumptions:\n148\t(1)~the rewriting procedure modifies only the target style dimension (verified by NLI, with residual false-negative rate bounded by triple indirect evidence);\n149\t(2)~position-controlled scoring eliminates order effects;\n150\t(3)~the BT model adequately represents the latent preference structure.\n151\tWe present a causal DAG in the supplementary material formalizing these assumptions.\n152\tWe use the term ``interventional measurement'' rather than ``causal inference'' to reflect the quasi-experimental nature of LLM-based text generation.\n153\t\n154\t% ============================================================\n155\t% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n156\t% ============================================================\n157\t\\section{Experiments}\n158\t\n159\t\\subsection{Cross-Judge Formality Bias}\\label{sec:crossjudge}\n160\t\n161\tWe apply the CSD pipeline on 100 formality counterfactual pairs from LLMBar, scored by four judges: GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, and Gemini-2.0-Flash.\n162\tTable~\\ref{tab:crossjudge} reports BT win probabilities, Likert ATEs, and Blindspot status.\n163\t\n164\t\\begin{table}[t]\n165\t\\centering\n166\t\\caption{Cross-judge formality bias on LLMBar. All four judges show significant pairwise bias (BT $p<0.001$) while Likert ATE remains non-significant (all $p>0.30$).}\n167\t\\label{tab:crossjudge}\n168\t\\small\n169\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n170\t\\toprule\n171\tJudge & BT & 95\\% CI & Likert $p$ & Blind \\\\\n172\t\\midrule\n173\tGPT-4o & 0.630 & [.558,.703] & .332 & \\cmark \\\\\n174\tQwen3-32B & 0.665 & [.585,.739] & .908 & \\cmark \\\\\n175\tClaude-3.5 & 0.645 & [.565,.725] & .641 & \\cmark \\\\\n176\tGemini-2.0 & 0.680 & [.595,.755] & .782 & \\cmark \\\\\n177\t\\bottomrule\n178\t\\end{tabular}\n179\t\\end{table}\n180\t\n181\tAll four judges significantly prefer formal over casual text in pairwise comparison (BT 0.630--0.680, all $p<0.001$), with narrow cross-judge variance (SD$\\approx$0.02).\n182\tLikert ATE is non-significant for all judges ($p$ ranging from 0.332 to 0.908), with tie rates exceeding 55\\% in every case.\n183\tThis confirms that formality bias is a systematic property of LLM judges, not an artifact of any individual model's training, and that the Likert Blindspot holds across architecturally diverse judges.\n184\t\n185\t\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Generalization}\\label{sec:crossbench}\n186\t\n187\tTo test whether formality bias generalizes beyond LLMBar, we generate 100 counterfactual pairs each from AlpacaEval and MT-Bench and score them with three judges (GPT-4o, Claude, Gemini).\n188\t\n189\t\\begin{table}[t]\n190\t\\centering\n191\t\\caption{Formality BT win probabilities across benchmarks and judges. All 9 cells significant ($^*p<0.05$). Cross-benchmark heterogeneity $p=0.42$ (Cochran's Q).}\n192\t\\label{tab:crossbench}\n193\t\\small\n194\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n195\t\\toprule\n196\tBenchmark & GPT-4o & Claude & Gemini \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\tLLMBar & 0.630$^*$ & 0.645$^*$ & 0.680$^*$ \\\\\n199\tAlpacaEval & 0.615$^*$ & 0.625$^*$ & 0.650$^*$ \\\\\n200\tMT-Bench & 0.590$^*$ & 0.610$^*$ & 0.640$^*$ \\\\\n201\t\\bottomrule\n202\t\\end{tabular}\n203\t\\end{table}\n204\t\n205\tAll 9 benchmark$\\times$judge cells show significant formality bias ($p<0.025$), with effect sizes ranging from 0.590 to 0.680.\n206\tThe cross-benchmark heterogeneity test is non-significant (Cochran's Q, $p=0.42$), indicating that formality bias does not depend on the source text distribution.\n207\tNLI pass rates on the new benchmarks remain high (AlpacaEval: 85\\%, MT-Bench: 82\\%), confirming counterfactual quality across domains.\n208\t\n209\t\\subsection{The Likert Blindspot}\\label{sec:blindspot}\n210\t\n211\tWe systematically compare Likert and pairwise sensitivity across all 18 judge$\\times$axis$\\times$benchmark cells tested.\n212\t\n213\t\\begin{table}[t]\n214\t\\centering\n215\t\\caption{Likert vs.\\ pairwise detection across 18 cells. Pairwise detects bias in 16/18 cells; Likert in only 2/18, both on the strongest axis (verbosity, BT$>$0.75).}\n216\t\\label{tab:blindspot_summary}\n217\t\\small\n218\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}\n219\t\\toprule\n220\t& Pairwise sig. & Likert sig. \\\\\n221\t\\midrule\n222\tTotal cells & 16/18 & 2/18 \\\\\n223\tBlindspot rate & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{87.5\\% (14/16)} \\\\\n224\tMedian sensitivity ratio & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{4.8$\\times$} \\\\\n225\t\\bottomrule\n226\t\\end{tabular}\n227\t\\end{table}\n228\t\n229\tTable~\\ref{tab:blindspot_summary} summarizes the detection rates.\n230\tPairwise comparison identifies significant style bias in 16 of 18 cells, while Likert scoring detects it in only 2 cells (both on the verbosity axis with BT$>$0.75).\n231\tThe median sensitivity ratio (pairwise effect size / Likert effect size) is 4.8$\\times$.\n232\t\n233\tThe two Likert-detectable cells occur for Gemini on verbosity (ATE$=$0.28, $p=0.038$) and Qwen on AlpacaEval verbosity (ATE$=$0.31, $p=0.029$), both corresponding to extreme pairwise effect sizes (BT$>$0.75) where the Likert tie rate drops below 60\\%.\n234\tThis boundary condition refines C2: Likert is effectively blind at moderate effect sizes (BT 0.60--0.70) and only occasionally detects the most extreme biases (BT$>$0.75).\n235\t\n236\tTable~\\ref{tab:blindspot} presents the complete 2$\\times$3 Blindspot evidence matrix for two primary judges across three axes.\n237\t\n238\t\\begin{table}[t]\n239\t\\centering\n240\t\\caption{Complete 2$\\times$3 Blindspot evidence matrix. $^*$Register cells are exploratory (BT marginally significant). All 6 cells confirm the Blindspot.}\n241\t\\label{tab:blindspot}\n242\t\\small\n243\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\n244\t\\toprule\n245\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{GPT-4o} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Qwen3-32B} \\\\\n246\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4}\\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n247\tAxis & BT & Lik.\\ $p$ & B & BT & Lik.\\ $p$ & B \\\\\n248\t\\midrule\n249\tFormal. & .630 & .332 & \\cmark & .665 & .908 & \\cmark \\\\\n250\tVerbose. & .863 & .358 & \\cmark & .773 & .179 & \\cmark \\\\\n251\tRegister & .585 & .612 & \\cmark$^*$ & .593 & .505 & \\cmark$^*$ \\\\\n252\t\\bottomrule\n253\t\\end{tabular}\n254\t\\end{table}\n255\t\n256\t\\paragraph{Scaled Replication.}\n257\tTo rule out underpowering as an explanation for Likert non-significance, we scale the primary formality experiment from $n=100$ to $n=400$ on the strongest judge-benchmark combination.\n258\t\n259\t\\begin{table}[t]\n260\t\\centering\n261\t\\caption{Scaled replication: $n=100$ vs.\\ $n=400$. Pairwise remains highly significant with tighter CI. Likert remains NS even at 4$\\times$ sample size. Binarized Likert reaches marginal significance at $n=400$, consistent with the power-curve prediction of $n\\approx462$.}\n262\t\\label{tab:scaled}\n263\t\\small\n264\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n265\t\\toprule\n266\tMetric & $n$=100 & $n$=400 & Note \\\\\n267\t\\midrule\n268\tBT win prob & 0.630 & 0.648 & Stable \\\\\n269\tBT CI width & 0.145 & 0.086 & $\\sqrt{n}$ scaling \\\\\n270\tBT $p$ & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}10^{-6}$ & Both sig. \\\\\n271\tLikert ATE $p$ & .332 & .185 & Still NS \\\\\n272\tBinarized Lik.\\ $p$ & .296 & .042 & Marginal \\\\\n273\t\\bottomrule\n274\t\\end{tabular}\n275\t\\end{table}\n276\t\n277\tAt $n=400$, pairwise BT remains highly significant ($p<10^{-6}$) with a stable point estimate (0.648 vs.\\ 0.630 at $n=100$) and tighter confidence interval (width 0.086 vs.\\ 0.145), confirming $\\sqrt{n}$ scaling.\n278\tLikert ATE remains non-significant ($p=0.185$) with a tie rate of 61\\%, demonstrating that the Blindspot is a measurement structure problem, not a sample size problem.\n279\tBinarized Likert (discarding ties) reaches marginal significance ($p=0.042$) at $n=400$, consistent with the power-curve prediction of $n\\approx462$ needed for 80\\% power.\n280\tThis validates the power-curve analysis and provides the strongest quantitative support for C2.\n281\t\n282\t\\subsection{GEE Confound Gradient}\\label{sec:gradient}\n283\t\n284\tGEE decomposition reveals a structured gradient across style axes, consistent across judges (Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n285\t\n286\t\\begin{table}[t]\n287\t\\centering\n288\t\\caption{GEE confound gradient across axes and judges. OR$_s$: style odds ratio; OR$_o$: originality odds ratio. Classification: CF (confound-free), MX (mixed), OD (originality-dominated). NS: $p>0.05$; $\\dagger$: $p<0.10$.}\n289\t\\label{tab:gee}\n290\t\\small\n291\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n292\t\\toprule\n293\t& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{GPT-4o} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen3-32B} \\\\\n294\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n295\tAxis & OR$_s$ & OR$_o$ & OR$_s$ & OR$_o$ \\\\\n296\t\\midrule\n297\tVerbos. (CF) & 10.5$^{***}$ & 0.62\\tiny{ NS} & 13.1$^{***}$ & 0.49\\tiny{ NS} \\\\\n298\tFormal. (MX) & 2.21$^{**}$ & 1.82$^{\\dagger}$ & 4.20$^{***}$ & 4.70$^{***}$ \\\\\n299\tRegister (OD) & 1.75$^{\\dagger}$ & 4.15$^{***}$ & 1.91$^{*}$ & 5.72$^{***}$ \\\\\n300\t\\bottomrule\n301\t\\end{tabular}\n302\t\\end{table}\n303\t\n304\t\\textbf{Verbosity} exhibits confound-free style bias: style OR exceeds 10 in both judges (GPT-4o: 10.5, Qwen3-32B: 13.1, both $p<10^{-7}$), while the originality OR is non-significant (0.62 and 0.49).\n305\tJudges overwhelmingly prefer verbose text purely on stylistic grounds, with no detectable preference for original over rewritten text.\n306\t\n307\t\\textbf{Formality} shows a mixed pattern: both style and originality ORs are significant for Qwen3-32B (4.20 and 4.70, both $p<0.001$), while for GPT-4o the style OR is significant (2.21, $p=0.010$) and the originality OR is marginal (1.82, $p=0.052$).\n308\tThe bias is real but partially confounded with originality preference.\n309\t\n310\t\\textbf{Register} is originality-dominated: originality OR exceeds 4 in both judges (GPT-4o: 4.15, Qwen3-32B: 5.72, both $p<0.0001$), while style OR is marginal or weakly significant (1.75 and 1.91).\n311\tThe observed pairwise preference on the register axis is primarily driven by preference for original text, not by the academic-vs-casual style shift itself.\n312\t\n313\tThis gradient has a coherent interpretation: content-preserving axes (verbosity adds words; formality changes surface markers) allow clean style bias detection, while content-altering axes (register shift changes the cognitive commitment and rhetorical stance of the text) introduce structural originality confounds.\n314\tThe gradient replicates across two architecturally distinct judges (proprietary GPT-4o vs.\\ open-source Qwen3-32B), supporting its generality.\n315\t\n316\t\\subsection{Robustness Controls}\\label{sec:robustness}\n317\t\n318\t\\paragraph{RATE Control: Quantifying Originality Confound.}\n319\tTo directly measure the originality confound, we run pairwise comparisons between original text and its double-rewrite (same style).\n320\tTable~\\ref{tab:rate} reports results.\n321\t\n322\t\\begin{table}[t]\n323\t\\centering\n324\t\\caption{RATE originality control. BT$_{\\text{orig}}$: original preference magnitude. BT$_{\\text{style}}$: style effect from Table~\\ref{tab:crossjudge}. Original preference is consistently smaller than style effect across all judges (heterogeneity $p=0.65$).}\n325\t\\label{tab:rate}\n326\t\\small\n327\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n328\t\\toprule\n329\tJudge & BT$_\\text{orig}$ & $p$ & BT$_\\text{style}$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n330\t\\midrule\n331\tGPT-4o & 0.565 & .085 & 0.630 & .065 \\\\\n332\tQwen3-32B & 0.540 & .220 & 0.665 & .125 \\\\\n333\tClaude & 0.555 & .140 & 0.645 & .090 \\\\\n334\tGemini & 0.575 & .052 & 0.680 & .105 \\\\\n335\t\\bottomrule\n336\t\\end{tabular}\n337\t\\end{table}\n338\t\n339\tOriginal preference BT (0.540--0.575) is consistently smaller than the style effect BT (0.630--0.680) across all four judges, with the difference ranging from 0.065 to 0.125.\n340\tNone of the originality-only comparisons reach significance at $\\alpha=0.05$ (though Gemini is marginal at $p=0.052$).\n341\tCross-judge heterogeneity is non-significant ($p=0.65$), indicating that original preference is a systematic but small confound.\n342\tThis cross-validates the GEE decomposition: the style component contributes the majority of the observed formality effect.\n343\t\n344\t\\paragraph{Rewriter Independence.}\n345\tTo rule out artifacts specific to the Qwen3-32B rewriter, we generate 100 formality counterfactual pairs using Llama-3.1-70B as an alternative rewriter.\n346\tCounterfactual quality is comparable (NLI pass rate: Llama 84\\% vs.\\ Qwen 88\\%, $p=0.31$; BERTScore: 0.875 vs.\\ 0.895).\n347\tDownstream pairwise results show no significant difference from Qwen-generated counterfactuals (BT difference $<$0.03, $p>0.40$ for both GPT-4o and Qwen judges).\n348\tThis confirms that bias detection is a property of the judge, not of the rewriter.\n349\t\n350\t\\paragraph{Register GEE Cross-Validation.}\n351\tGPT-4o scoring on register counterfactuals yields BT$=$0.585 ($p=0.042$) with GEE decomposition: academic OR$=$1.75 ($p=0.072$), originality OR$=$4.15 ($p<0.0001$).\n352\tThis replicates the originality-dominated classification observed in Qwen3-32B (Table~\\ref{tab:gee}), confirming the confound gradient's cross-judge consistency.\n353\t\n354\t% ============================================================\n355\t% 5. DISCUSSION\n356\t% ============================================================\n357\t\\section{Discussion}\\label{sec:discussion}\n358\t\n359\t\\paragraph{Why Does the Blindspot Occur?}\n360\tThe Likert Blindspot arises from two reinforcing mechanisms.\n361\tFirst, Likert scoring produces high tie rates (55--65\\% across judges and axes): when both texts in a counterfactual pair receive the same absolute rating, no preference signal is captured.\n362\tSecond, absolute scoring inherently has lower discriminability than forced comparison for subtle quality differences, a phenomenon well-established in psychometrics \\citep{decoster2011,stevens1946}.\n363\tThe scaled replication at $n=400$ confirms that this is structural, not a power issue: Likert ATE remains non-significant while pairwise BT achieves $p<10^{-6}$.\n364\t\n365\t\\paragraph{Implications for Existing Benchmarks.}\n366\tOur findings suggest that Likert-based evaluations of LLM judge reliability may systematically underestimate style bias.\n367\tThe RATE protocol \\citep{rate2025}, which uses Likert ATE as its primary metric, may miss moderate-strength style biases that pairwise comparison would detect.\n368\tWe recommend that future judge evaluation frameworks include pairwise comparison as a complementary sensitivity check.\n369\t\n370\t\\paragraph{The Confound Gradient as Practitioner Guidance.}\n371\tThe GEE confound gradient provides actionable structure for bias auditing.\n372\tWhen evaluating a new style axis, practitioners should first assess whether the style transformation preserves content (via NLI verification), then apply GEE decomposition to determine whether detected bias reflects genuine style preference or originality confound.\n373\tContent-preserving axes (formality, verbosity) yield interpretable bias estimates; content-altering axes (register, tone) require careful decomposition before conclusions can be drawn.\n374\t\n375\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n376\tOur study has several limitations.\n377\tThe CSD pipeline relies on LLM-generated counterfactuals, which may introduce subtle content changes not captured by NLI verification (false-negative rate bounded but non-zero).\n378\tWe examine three style axes; other dimensions (e.g., tone, hedging, lexical sophistication) remain unexplored.\n379\tOur sample sizes ($n=100$--$400$ per cell) provide sufficient power for moderate effects but may miss very small biases.\n380\tThe GEE decomposition assumes a logit-linear relationship between style/originality predictors and preference, which may not hold in all cases.\n381\t\n382\t% ============================================================\n383\t% 6. CONCLUSION\n384\t% ============================================================\n385\t\\section{Conclusion}\n386\t\n387\tWe present the CSD pipeline and identify the Likert Blindspot: a systematic failure of standard Likert scoring to detect style biases that pairwise comparison reveals across 4 judges, 3 axes, and 3 benchmarks.\n388\tGEE decomposition uncovers a confound gradient, from confound-free verbosity bias (OR$>$10) through mixed formality to originality-dominated register, that replicates across judges and provides structured guidance for bias auditing.\n389\tThese findings call for pairwise comparison as a standard complement to Likert scoring in LLM judge evaluation, and for per-axis confound decomposition when interpreting style bias measurements.\n390\t\n391\t% ============================================================\n392\t% REFERENCES\n393\t% ============================================================\n394\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n395\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n396\t\n397\t\\bibitem[Bradley and Terry(1952)]{bradleyterry1952}\n398\tR.~A. Bradley and M.~E. Terry.\n399\t\\newblock Rank analysis of incomplete block designs: {I}. {T}he method of paired comparisons.\n400\t\\newblock \\textit{Biometrika}, 39(3/4):324--345, 1952.\n401\t\n402\t\\bibitem[Chen et~al.(2024)]{lcalpacaeval2025}\n403\tY.~Chen, Y.~Li, and others.\n404\t\\newblock Length-controlled {AlpacaEval}: A simple way to debias automatic evaluators.\n405\t\\newblock In \\textit{Proc.\\ ICLR}, 2025.\n406\t\n407\t\\bibitem[DeCoster et~al.(2011)]{decoster2011}\n408\tJ.~DeCoster, A.~Gallucci, and A.~Iselin.\n409\t\\newblock Best practices for using median splits, artificial categorization, and their continuous alternatives.\n410\t\\newblock \\textit{Journal of Experimental Psychopathology}, 2(2):197--209, 2011.\n411\t\n412\t\\bibitem[Feinstein(1990)]{feinstein1990}\n413\tA.~R. Feinstein.\n414\t\\newblock {``The unit fragility index'': an additional appraisal of ``statistical significance'' for a contrast of two proportions.}\n415\t\\newblock \\textit{J.\\ Clinical Epidemiology}, 43(2):201--209, 1990.\n416\t\n417\t\\bibitem[Hoenig and Heisey(2001)]{hoenig2001}\n418\tJ.~M. Hoenig and D.~M. Heisey.\n419\t\\newblock The abuse of power: the pervasive fallacy of power calculations for data analysis.\n420\t\\newblock \\textit{The American Statistician}, 55(1):19--24, 2001.\n421\t\n422\t\\bibitem[Liang and Zeger(1986)]{liang1986}\n423\tK.-Y. Liang and S.~L. Zeger.\n424\t\\newblock Longitudinal data analysis using generalized linear models.\n425\t\\newblock \\textit{Biometrika}, 73(1):13--22, 1986.\n426\t\n427\t\\bibitem[Pan et~al.(2025)]{sosbench2025}\n428\tJ.~Pan, K.~Sun, and others.\n429\t\\newblock {SOS-Bench}: Systematic evaluation of {LLM}-as-judge with style-oriented sensitivity.\n430\t\\newblock In \\textit{Proc.\\ ICLR}, 2025.\n431\t\n432\t\\bibitem[Stevens(1946)]{stevens1946}\n433\tS.~S. Stevens.\n434\t\\newblock On the theory of scales of measurement.\n435\t\\newblock \\textit{Science}, 103(2684):677--680, 1946.\n436\t\n437\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2025)]{rate2025}\n438\tZ.~Wang, S.~Debnath, and others.\n439\t\\newblock {RATE}: Regression-adjusted treatment effect estimation for {LLM} evaluation.\n440\t\\newblock In \\textit{Proc.\\ ICML}, 2025.\n441\t\n442\t\\bibitem[Wu et~al.(2025)]{counterfactual_rhetorical2025}\n443\tM.~Wu, J.~Liu, and others.\n444\t\\newblock Counterfactual rhetorical evaluation of {LLM} judges.\n445\t\\newblock \\textit{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n446\t\n447\t\\bibitem[Ye et~al.(2026)]{casse2026}\n448\tS.~Ye, X.~Chen, and others.\n449\t\\newblock {CASSE}: Comprehensive assessment of style sensitivity in evaluation.\n450\t\\newblock \\textit{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n451\t\n452\t\\bibitem[Zeng et~al.(2024)]{zeng2024}\n453\tZ.~Zeng, J.~Yu, and others.\n454\t\\newblock Evaluating large language models at evaluating instruction following.\n455\t\\newblock In \\textit{Proc.\\ ACL}, 2024.\n456\t\n457\t\\end{thebibliography}\n458\t\n459\t\\end{document}\n460\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FJcotXASHd7kdeVDZSpUAd","session_id":"e3b839b6-f829-41eb-a911-4574b0a14ec9","uuid":"028b4d0c-6822-4ea7-9136-0d680421914e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:54:09.081Z"}
